Q: How to use images when it comes to SQL databases? A: <h2>Question #1: How to store image in a MySQL table</h2>
<p>MySQL supports <strong>blob</strong> data types, allowing you to store images or any other kind of binary data.
There are 4 such types which only vary in sizes:</p>
<ul>
<li>TINYBLOB : up to 255 bytes, best for keeping very tiny icons or flags</li>
<li>BLOB : up 64 KB, for things like small GIF/JPEG images, normal size documents</li>
<li>MEDIUMBLOB : up to 16 MB, enough to keep most PDF files and photo images or music MP3s</li>
<li>LONGBLOB : up to 4 GB, should be enough to hold videos</li>
</ul>
<p>So far there's no larger blob type available in MySQL yet.</p>
<p>See also:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/blob.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/blob.html</a></li>
<li><a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/storage-requirements.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/storage-requirements.html</a></li>
</ul>
<h4>Store image to blob column</h4>
<p>To create a table with a blob column enough to keep a normal size image:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>CREATE TABLE data_table (
  id   INT AUTO_INCREMENT PRIMARY KEY,
  name VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL, -- eg. 'family-gathering.jpg'
  mime VARCHAR(80) NOT NULL,  -- eg. 'image/jpeg'
  data MEDIUMBLOB NOT NULL
);
</code></pre>
<p>If your image is already stored in a location where DB server can directly access to, this is the statement to store the image data into DB:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>INSERT INTO data_table(name, mime, data)
VALUES('family-gathering.jpg', 'image/jpg', LOAD_FILE('/path-to-the-file/family-gathering.jpg'));
</code></pre>
<p>But oftentimes that is not the case and the data is actually only available on the application server, not DB server.
You can pass the data just like ordinary column data in your SQL query. The following is a possible example code written in C#:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using System.IO;
using MySql.Data.MySqlClient;

// prepare variables host, db, account, pw, dirPath, fileName...

byte[] data = File.ReadAllBytes(Path.Combine(dirPath, fileName));
using (var conn = new MySqlConnection($&quot;Server={host};Database={db};User ID={account};Password={pw};&quot;))
{
    conn.Open();
    string sql = &quot;&quot;&quot;
                 INSERT INTO data_table(name, mime, data)
                 VALUES (@name, @mime, @data)
                 &quot;&quot;&quot;;
    using var cmd = new MySqlCommand(sql, conn);
    cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(&quot;@name&quot;, fileName);
    cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(&quot;@mime&quot;, &quot;image/jpg&quot;);
    cmd.Parameters.Add(&quot;@data&quot;, MySqlDbType.MediumBlob).Value = data;
    cmd.ExecuteNonQuery();
}
</code></pre>
<h4>Retrieve image from blob column</h4>
<p>Just like any other types of data, like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>SELECT name, mime, data
FROM data_table
WHERE id = 1234;
</code></pre>
<p>Or if you want to store it on certain location in DB server:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>SELECT data
INTO DUMPFILE '/path-to-the-output-file/saved-family-gathering.jpg'
FROM data_table
WHERE id = 1234;
</code></pre>
<p>As explained in:</p>
<ul>
<li><a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/select-into.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/select-into.html</a></li>
</ul>
<p>But generally this is not the preferred solution, not only it saves the data on DB server, it's also saved with a <strong>fixed</strong> name in certain directory path.
And currently due to MySQL's limitation we cannot use a variable in the <code>INTO DUMPFILE</code> clause.
That means in a multi-user DB server it is highly likely to have a collision problem.</p>
<p>The preferred way is to use the ordinary <code>SELECT</code> without <code>INTO DUMPFILE</code> and the application code should receive the blob data and store it on application side. The following is a possible C# example code.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using System.IO;
using MySql.Data.MySqlClient;

// prepare variables host, db, account, pw, id, dirPath...

using (var conn = new MySqlConnection($&quot;Server={host};Database={db};User ID={account};Password={pw};&quot;))
{
    conn.Open();
    string sql = &quot;&quot;&quot;
                 SELECT name, data
                 FROM data_table
                 WHERE id = @id
                 &quot;&quot;&quot;;
    using var cmd = new MySqlCommand(sql, conn);
    cmd.Parameters.AddWithValue(&quot;@id&quot;, id);
    using var reader = cmd.ExecuteReader();
    if (reader.Read())
    {
        string name = reader.GetString(&quot;name&quot;);
        byte[] blob = (byte[])reader[&quot;data&quot;];
        File.WriteAllBytes(Path.Combine(outputPath, name), blob);
    }
    else
    {
        Console.WriteLine($&quot;No record found with id={id}&quot;);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<h2>Question #2: Store image on an external storage</h2>
<p>It is quite common practice to store large data files, such as images, outside of a database server. DB servers are not the most efficient storage solution for such task. Instead, you can use a service specifically designed for the purpose and AWS S3 is one such option. However, be prepared that cloud storage services would charge for storage fees one way or another.</p>
<p>If you decide to go this route, you need to design the table columns to store the image's identity info such as the resource path on the storage service and the resource ID. Also the storage service typically would require an authentication key or token so your application can access the data. It is strongly recommended <strong>not</strong> to keep this authentication info in DB to avoid potential security risks. Instead, your program should be designed so that the authentication credentials are retrieved from a secure source or obtained dynamically from the storage service as a one‑time access key or token.</p>

Q: The underlying connection was closed when using a WSDL web service A: <p>I tried service on my playground project. It works well.</p>
<p>You can try increase inner channel timeout value. And retry again please. If you using service pattern, your service should construct it with timeout. Inner channel can be null if client configuration is wrong or bad. Handle it.</p>
<pre><code>using TransitTimeService;

transitPortTypeClient transitPortTypeClient = new(endpointConfiguration: TransitTimeService.transitPortTypeClient.EndpointConfiguration.transitPortType);

transitPortTypeClient.InnerChannel.OperationTimeout = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(15);

serviceRequest serviceRequest = new()
{
    shipDate = DateTime.Now
};

serviceRequest1 serviceRequest1 = new()
{
    auth = new AuthenticationType()
    {
        user = &quot;test&quot;,
        password = &quot;test&quot;
    },
    serviceRequest = serviceRequest
};

var result = await transitPortTypeClient.calculateAsync(serviceRequest1);

Console.WriteLine(result.serviceResult.Item);

await transitPortTypeClient.CloseAsync();
</code></pre>
